# ═══════════ WHAT THE EDGE SENDS BACK WITH EVERY FILE ═══════════════════════
#
# ── WHY THIS FILE EXISTS ──
# Measured on the live site, 2026-08-19, with Lighthouse:
#
#     curl -I https://www.maginhawagroup.co.uk/_next/static/chunks/webpack-94e2912f6b6bd973.js
#     cache-control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
#
# EVERY asset the Worker serves came back with that, including the ones whose
# filenames already contain a content hash. `webpack-94e2912f6b6bd973.js` can
# never change without its URL changing — that is the entire point of the hash
# — and yet the browser was told to check with the server before reusing it on
# every single navigation. Lighthouse costed the whole set at 9,561 KiB, of
# which 9,783,534 bytes is one file: `/videos/belly-hero.mp4`.
#
# The revalidation is not a re-download — a 304 sends no body — but it IS a
# blocking round trip per asset before anything renders or plays, and it is
# bytes on any cache that cannot revalidate. There is no reason to pay it.
#
# ── WHY IT WORKS, AND HOW WE KNOW ──
# Same mechanism as `public/_redirects`, and it is worth stating once for both:
# `output: "export"` copies `public/` into `out/` verbatim, `wrangler.toml`
# publishes `out/` as the Worker's `[assets]` directory, and the static-asset
# runtime reads `_headers` and `_redirects` out of the published root. That
# chain is not assumed — the redirects are live right now:
#
#     curl -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code} %{redirect_url}' .../join-us
#     308 https://www.maginhawagroup.co.uk/careers
#
# ⚠️ SO THIS FILE IS ONLY IN FORCE ONCE IT IS DEPLOYED. Nothing local reads it;
# `next dev` and `npx serve out` both ignore it entirely. Verify it with curl
# against the deployed origin, never against localhost.
#
# ── THE RULES BELOW DO NOT OVERLAP, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE ──
# Cloudflare applies EVERY matching rule, so two rules that both set
# `Cache-Control` on the same path leave the winner to precedence rather than
# to the reader. The split here is by concern instead: `/*` carries security
# headers and no caching, and the caching rules sit on path prefixes that are
# mutually exclusive. Add a rule and keep that property.


# ── EVERY RESPONSE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
/*
  # Stop the browser second-guessing a declared Content-Type. The press wall
  # serves user-supplied-looking SVG out of /press-logo/; sniffing is exactly
  # the vector that turns a mislabelled file into script.
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff

  # Send the full URL to ourselves, the bare origin to anyone else. Cloudinary
  # and Typekit have no business knowing which page a reader is on.
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin

  # This site asks for none of these. Declaring that explicitly means an
  # injected script cannot ask on its behalf either.
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), interest-cohort=()

  # Two years, subdomains included, and preload-eligible. The site is HTTPS-only
  # already; this closes the first plaintext request that a typed hostname
  # would otherwise make.
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload

  # Nothing here is meant to be framed. `frame-ancestors` in the CSP below is
  # the modern spelling and the one that actually governs; XFO is kept for the
  # older agents that only understand it. They agree on purpose.
  X-Frame-Options: DENY

  # Give the page its own browsing-context group.
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin

  # ── THE CONTENT SECURITY POLICY, AND AN HONEST NOTE ON ITS SCRIPT RULE ──
  # The origins are enumerated from a real network trace of the home page,
  # not guessed. In full, the site talks to: itself, res.cloudinary.com (every
  # photograph, via lib/cloudinaryLoader.ts), use.typekit.net (the Contralto
  # kit's stylesheet AND its woff2 files), p.typekit.net (the kit's second
  # stylesheet hop), and `data:` (the inline SVGs in the compiled CSS).
  #
  # ⚠️ `script-src` IS THE WEAK LINE AND IT IS WEAK ON PURPOSE.
  # `'unsafe-inline'` is not a shortcut here, it is forced: Next inlines the
  # hydration payload and three JSON-LD blocks directly into the document, and
  # the nonce that would normally replace this needs a server to generate one
  # per response. Under `output: "export"` there is no server — the HTML is a
  # file on disk, so every visitor would share one nonce, which is the same as
  # having none. `'unsafe-eval'` is likewise present for Cloudflare's own
  # /cdn-cgi/challenge-platform script, which we do not control and must not
  # break.
  #
  # What is left still earns its place, and it is not nothing: an injected
  # <script src> pointing anywhere off-origin is refused, `object-src 'none'`
  # kills plugin-based execution, `base-uri 'self'` blocks the <base> tag
  # rewrite that turns every relative URL into an attacker's, `form-action`
  # stops a planted form posting off-site, and `frame-ancestors` is the
  # clickjacking control. Do not read this policy as XSS-proof. Read it as the
  # strongest policy a statically exported site can actually enforce.
# ── GOOGLE ANALYTICS 4 AND THE THREE DIRECTIVES IT NEEDS ──
# Added when GA4 went in. All three are required and each fails DIFFERENTLY,
# which is why they are listed rather than folded into one wildcard:
#   script-src   www.googletagmanager.com  — loads gtag.js itself. Without
#                it the tag never runs and the console shows a CSP refusal.
#   connect-src  *.google-analytics.com, *.analytics.google.com,
#                www.googletagmanager.com — where the measurement beacons
#                are POSTed. ⚠️ THIS IS THE ONE THAT FAILS QUIETLY: with
#                script-src alone, gtag loads, initialises, reports no error
#                a casual look would catch, and every event is blocked in
#                transit. The symptom is a live tag and an empty dashboard.
#                The region-specific endpoints are why these are wildcards —
#                GA4 sends to region1.google-analytics.com and friends.
#   img-src      googletagmanager.com, google-analytics.com — the legacy
#                pixel fallback, used when fetch/beacon is unavailable.
#
# ⚠️ NOTHING HERE MAKES A REQUEST BEFORE CONSENT. The CSP grants permission;
# components/Analytics.tsx decides whether to use it, and does not render
# the script tag at all until the visitor has accepted. Widening the policy
# is not the same as loading the tag.
# ── THE MARKETING PIXELS: META, TIKTOK, GOOGLE ADS ──
# Added alongside the GA4 entries above. Each vendor needs THREE directives
# and each of the three fails in a different way, which is why none of this
# is folded into a wildcard:
#   script-src  connect.facebook.net, analytics.tiktok.com,
#               googleadservices.com — loads each library. A miss here is
#               LOUD: a CSP refusal in the console and no pixel at all.
#   connect-src facebook.com, *.tiktok.com, google.com — where the events
#               are POSTed. ⚠️ THIS IS THE ONE THAT FAILS QUIETLY, exactly
#               as it does for GA4: the pixel loads, initialises, reports
#               nothing wrong, and every event is blocked in transit. The
#               symptom is a live pixel and an empty Events Manager.
#   img-src     facebook.com, googleads.g.doubleclick.net, google.com,
#               google.co.uk — the tracking-pixel fallback each platform
#               drops back to when fetch/beacon is unavailable. google.co.uk
#               is not redundant: Google Ads pings the LOCAL ccTLD for
#               conversion attribution, and a UK advertiser's hits go there.
#
# ⚠️ WIDENING THE POLICY IS NOT LOADING THE TAG. Nothing here makes a
# request before consent; the CSP grants permission and
# components/MarketingPixels.tsx decides whether to use it, rendering no
# script at all until `marketing` is granted.
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' 'unsafe-eval' https://www.googletagmanager.com https://www.googleadservices.com https://connect.facebook.net https://analytics.tiktok.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://use.typekit.net https://p.typekit.net; font-src 'self' https://use.typekit.net data:; img-src 'self' https://res.cloudinary.com https://www.googletagmanager.com https://www.google-analytics.com https://googleads.g.doubleclick.net https://www.google.com https://www.google.co.uk https://www.facebook.com https://analytics.tiktok.com data: blob:; media-src 'self' blob:; connect-src 'self' https://res.cloudinary.com https://p.typekit.net https://www.google-analytics.com https://*.google-analytics.com https://*.analytics.google.com https://www.googletagmanager.com https://www.google.com https://connect.facebook.net https://www.facebook.com https://analytics.tiktok.com https://*.tiktok.com; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; upgrade-insecure-requests


# ── CONTENT-HASHED BUILD OUTPUT ────────────────────────────────────────────
# `immutable` is only ever correct when the filename changes with the bytes,
# and here it does: Next puts a content hash in every chunk, stylesheet and
# font under this prefix, and a rebuild that changes a file changes its URL.
# A year is the maximum the spec allows and there is no staleness risk, so
# there is no reason to ask for less. This is the rule that removes the
# revalidation round trip from the critical path on every repeat visit.
/_next/static/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable


# ── THE FILM, THE PHOTOGRAPHS AND THE MARKS ────────────────────────────────
# ⚠️ NOT `immutable`, AND THE DIFFERENCE MATTERS. These filenames are stable —
# `/videos/belly-hero.mp4` stays `/videos/belly-hero.mp4` when the cut changes
# — so `immutable` would pin a superseded film in readers' caches for as long
# as it was set, with no way to reach them. Thirty days with a normal
# revalidation is the trade: the repeat visit is free, and a replaced asset is
# picked up within the month rather than never.
#
# Thirty days is also what Cloudinary itself returns for the photographs
# (`max-age=2592000`, checked), so the two halves of the media split now agree
# instead of one being a hundred times more cacheable than the other.
#
# ⚠️ IF YOU REPLACE A FILE AND NEED IT LIVE TODAY, change its name and change
# the reference. That is the only reliable cache-bust; purging the edge does
# not reach a browser that already has it.
/videos/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000

/images/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000

/press-logo/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000

/logo/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000

/menu/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000


# ── THE HTML IS DELIBERATELY NOT CACHED ────────────────────────────────────
# Not an oversight and not a leftover. The pages are the one thing that must
# change the moment a deploy lands: they carry the copy, the metadata, the
# JSON-LD and the hashed URLs of everything above. Caching them is how a
# reader ends up on last week's page requesting this week's chunks. The
# assets they point at are immutable, so the document is the ONLY thing worth
# revalidating — which is exactly the shape this leaves.
/*.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
